module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.LogIdealAlgebraizationData

/-!
# LogIdealImageExistence

A step of logarithmic image-ideal algebraization.
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-ideal-image-existence).
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
universe u
open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite
namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The image ideal of a nonempty coherent log ideal has a unique finite-type
Zariski algebraization, with equality as ideals rather than merely an isomorphism.
Source: ILO, Exposé VIII 3.3.7 and 3.4.2; Stacks, Tags 01IB, 01IC, 03DX and 05VG
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-ideal-image-existence).

Proof: Choose étale-local fine charts and finite nonempty ideal generators E.
At a geometric point every ideal germ is e*m, so its structural image is
alpha(e)*alpha(m). Thus the image ideal is locally the image of the finite free
map with coefficients alpha(e); the reverse containment follows since the
charted generators belong to the ideal stalk. On affine charts this is the
tilde module of the ring ideal generated by those coefficients. The
quasi-coherent image theorem identifies it with the Zariski image of the same
free map. The tilde counit respects the inclusion by naturality. Exact étale
association preserves that image: it preserves the free epimorphism and the
monic inclusion, using the quasi-coherent cokernel. These local presentations
make the small-étale image ideal site quasi-coherent and of finite type.
Apply the Zariski/étale quasi-coherent equivalence and its inverse, restriction
to open subschemes, also to its inclusion into the structure sheaf. On each
open the resulting module consists of precisely the functions in the image
ideal; hence it is a genuine ideal subsheaf and determines ideal sheaf data K.
The finite-type comparison reflects the finite free local epimorphisms, so K
is of finite type. If L has the same image-ideal sections, restriction to open
subschemes gives equality of the ideals of functions on each affine open.
Extensionality of ideal sheaf data gives L = K. No Noetherian or saturation
hypothesis is used. The construction and uniqueness also apply to the empty
scheme and to the unit or zero structural image ideal. -/
theorem logIdeal_image_exists_unique {X : Scheme.{u}} (M : EtaleLogStructure X)
    (hM : M.IsFine) (I : M.IdealFamily) (hI : M.IsNonemptyFineCoherentIdeal I) :
    ∃ K : X.IdealSheafData,
      Scheme.Modules.IsQuasiCoherentFiniteType K.toModules ∧
      M.IsImageIdealAlgebraization I K ∧
      ∀ L : X.IdealSheafData, M.IsImageIdealAlgebraization I L → L = K := by sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
